5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:53,360
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard, and my guest today is Patrick Falzen, co founder of the App Shop, helping companies of all sizes build, grow and monetize exceptional apps.

7
00:00:53,920 --> 00:01:04,960
On the podcast, I talk with Patrick about estimating the revenue potential of an app, crafting an exit strategy, and why LTV is such a terrible metric.

8
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:08,560
Hey Patrick, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

9
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:09,680
Thank you for having me.

10
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:12,480
Big fan of the podcast and big, big fan of RevenueCat.

11
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:14,880
Yeah, super excited to have you on the podcast.

12
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:18,080
And I wanted to kick things off talking about your time at Mosaic.

13
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:19,200
I think that's a good framing.

14
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:24,640
It's kind of where you learn the ropes as far as subscription app businesses go.

15
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:32,800
And Mosaic did a lot of really interesting stuff and built a pretty great business, famously, recently, got sold off to Bending Spoons.

16
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:41,440
But I wanted to talk a little bit about your experience there, kind of what brought you there and what your role was as a context for what we're going to talk about throughout the rest of the podcast episode.

17
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:48,000
Yeah, the brief background to me is my career has been roughly divided between investing and operating roles.

18
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:50,880
And Mosaic was actually the place where I made that transition.

19
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:56,720
So I got introduced to Mosaic when it was actually just Avalon back in the day for folks familiar with that brand.

20
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:00,080
Avalon had made really early to the mobile app ecosystem.

21
00:02:00,640 --> 00:02:04,640
Our parent company was a company called IAC had acquired them when everything was still paid downloads.

22
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:09,920
And so Avalon, we owned it when it went through that transition from paid downloads to subscriptions.

23
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:13,760
And we saw phenomenal growth and a ton of opportunity on the other side of that.

24
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:17,040
And this was at a time when I was still doing mergers and acquisitions for IAC.

25
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:19,600
While I was on the team, I got introduced to the Avalon team.

26
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:20,560
They had a big vision.

27
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:32,160
They felt like they really unlocked something special in terms of understanding and being able to capitalize on that in-app subscription opportunity and wanted to go out and build a big portfolio of other apps and other app developers.

28
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:45,680
So I originally came in to help them execute on that from an MA perspective, first through the acquisitions of iTranslate and Teltek, iTranslate being the developers of iTranslate, Teltek, the developers of RoboKiller, plus some others, but RoboKiller was the flagship product there.

29
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:48,560
And then a number of other smaller deals on the other side of that.

30
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:58,000
So I got involved in Mosaic and then within a couple of years, I actually ended up running both iTranslate and Teltek as the general manager of those two businesses while still doing the MA for Mosaic.

31
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:05,200
So I got to see the landscape, I think, from a couple of different lenses, one, both from the investing and the operating side over a multiyear period.

32
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:09,200
And then also really got to ride that growth of the mobile app industry.

33
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:11,520
Again, we were pretty early to subscriptions.

34
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:19,200
And when there was kind of like just infinite growth, it felt like in those early days all the way to through to now where I think it's a much more mature ecosystem.

35
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:20,560
Everyone's on subscriptions.

36
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:23,040
There's a lot more defined best practices around how to do it.

37
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:29,840
And then, frankly, I also think in some ways growth is maybe harder than it used to be and it takes more execution than it used to.

38
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:31,680
So it's been a really fun journey.

39
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:34,640
And all I would say is that Mosaic, we do feel like we accomplished a lot.

40
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:39,200
We felt like we built one of the leading portfolios in the space had a great exit to bending spoons.

41
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:41,360
Hope they do well with the portfolio on the other side of it.

42
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:43,040
I'm excited for what comes next.

43
00:03:43,360 --> 00:04:08,320
Yeah, it must have been a fascinating time going from that single kind of portfolio of apps with Apple on to then acquiring and getting to kind of peek inside a lot of app businesses in the MA role and then actually become an operator over Teltek, which I know RoboKiller was one of the kind of flagship apps of the Mosaic portfolio while still doing additional MA along the way.

44
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:19,840
So it's like you were both kind of seeing what was going on inside these other businesses that Mosaic might want to buy, but then also like implementing those strategies within the existing portfolio company.

45
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:21,920
So you just have such a unique perspective.

46
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:32,320
And again, that's why I think it's going to be a really fascinating chat today to kind of learn from those experiences, you know, kind of one of the top and one of the early movers in this subscription app space.

47
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:36,160
So the first thing I wanted to talk about is market sizing.

48
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:44,640
And this is something you must have thought a lot about when you had your MA hat on was how big is this market going to be for this app?

49
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:46,400
You know, how much can we afford to pay?

50
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:47,840
How much do we think this is going to grow?

51
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:51,680
Because, you know, famously Mosaic didn't just buy up a bunch of apps.

52
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:57,840
Like there's a lot of companies now that'll just buy any app that's doing, you know, 10,000 a month, 100,000 a month.

53
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,560
And it seems like there's not as focused a strategy.

54
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:06,640
But with Mosaic, there was, it seems like, a lot more thoughtful approach to, like, trying to buy apps that could be big.

55
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:16,400
And then the other thing, I imagine as general manager at Rubikiller, then you also had to think about, like, what's the opportunity of any new feature we build?

56
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:21,200
And those are probably two very similar problem spaces to think about.

57
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:24,000
Like we have this existing app that has a market cap.

58
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:26,160
Is this secondary product market fit?

59
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:27,520
Should this be a new app?

60
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:29,680
And there's so much intertwined with those two.

61
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:31,200
So I want to kind of lump those together.

62
00:05:31,440 --> 00:05:32,320
But let's kick it off.

63
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:36,240
Like, how did you think about acquiring a new app or going into a new line of business?

64
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:40,480
Like, how to really understand that potential TAM for that app?

65
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:41,120
Yeah.

66
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:44,160
So we did think about it across a couple of different lenses.

67
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:45,680
So one was just market size.

68
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:46,640
You are right.

69
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:48,240
We went for depth.

70
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:55,440
So we needed to believe that you could have a product that did tens of millions of dollars of revenue was kind of a rough benchmark we had for ourselves.

71
00:05:55,920 --> 00:06:09,760
The main reason we did that, and I think one of the challenges when you go more downstream than that is., you can easily end up with a portfolio of like fifty to a hundred apps that each do, you know, a couple of hundred thousand dollars of revenue and your overall P and L looks good.

72
00:06:10,080 --> 00:06:13,040
But it is really complex managing that many apps, one.

73
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:18,400
And two, what it probably means is you're very rarely actually investing in each of those apps.

74
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:24,000
Maybe there's a couple you do, but you have this long tail of kind of zombie products where they're making revenue for me, for you.

75
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:25,440
And maybe that makes financial sense.

76
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:30,960
But it was hard for us to justify that we were truly satisfying those users or providing value on the other side of that.

77
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:44,400
And that's why we tried to shy away from that and really wanted to buy products where we felt like, or build products where we felt like this was a durable product, a durable category, we can invest in it and actually provide value to our users on a continuous basis and be rewarded for that.

78
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:45,840
So we did look for depth.

79
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:55,520
Now, on the flip side of that, I do think there's a double edged sword on your TAM, where I could also think about like you should think about how big do you really want to play in because it gets into the second lens which is competition.

80
00:06:55,760 --> 00:07:10,400
And typically the bigger markets are going to have more competition and you have to think about how you think about those tradeoffs between those two things, meaning a big market is great only if you can take a substantial share of that market or a specific share of that market.

81
00:07:10,880 --> 00:07:16,400
If it is so competitive that you can't actually garner market share, it's not actually valuable to you.

82
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:20,720
So we thought about size and competition as a balance between each other.

83
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:33,280
And I think there's a little bit of a Goldilocks approach you can take to those things of markets that are big enough to create enduring products and businesses, but you could still be maybe a relative big fish in a smaller pond.

84
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:35,840
And that's not necessarily a terrible place for you to play.

85
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:42,720
Do you have some concrete examples of like, I mean, let's take RoboKiller maybe as an example of something that Mosaic did buy.

86
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:58,720
So what was the thought there on like how big that market was and the thesis behind playing in that market versus and then maybe an example of a market that was maybe too competitive where you're where you looked at an acquisition and felt like this is too big a space to compete in.

87
00:07:59,200 --> 00:07:59,280
Yeah.

88
00:07:59,440 --> 00:08:04,240
So for the RoboKiller example, the basic thesis there is it's a broadly applicable product.

89
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:06,960
Everyone gets spam calls or spam techs to some degree.

90
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:17,280
There are certain demographics that care more about solving that problem than others, but we got comfortable that it was a broadly appearing appealing product that could generate again tens of millions of dollars of revenue.

91
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:20,320
And then we looked at the competitive landscape and RoboKiller was the leader.

92
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:29,200
You had carriers are somewhat relevant here, but they're not really invested in solving the solution for a variety of reasons that are probably more of a rabbit hole than worth getting into here.

93
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:32,880
So we looked at this as a problem that needs to be solved by independent tools.

94
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:35,120
This is the leading independent tool.

95
00:08:35,440 --> 00:08:39,200
There aren't major players entering this space from adjacent categories.

96
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:42,480
So again, good size, good competitive dynamics.

97
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:47,360
I think one you can think about in the other direction, categories that we never got in ourselves.

98
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:49,760
Two examples that come to mind are VPNs.

99
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:54,640
VPN is a massive category, but there's also already a lot of people making a lot of money there.

100
00:08:54,800 --> 00:09:00,640
So if you want to enter that category, you have to be prepared to spend a lot of money on user acquisition to be able to.

101
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:07,760
to be able to garner market share because everyone else is spending a lot of money on user acquisition and if you don't play that game, there is no game to play.

102
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:14,000
So that was one we shied away from because we said the unique economics are not going to make sense for us long term if we have to be so reliant on aggressive paid marketing.

103
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:40,560
Slightly different lens but similar rationale is we never got into personal budgeting or finance for a similar but slightly different reason in the sense that we thought the adjacent competitors in banks and more traditional financial institutions made that challenging long term because similarly when you go out to acquire users, if you think about like what you're actually acquiring them for, you're acquiringing them for financial management or budgeting or like their money and how should they manage that.

104
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:49,360
Those are the same desires and objectives and keywords to be very tactical that like a bank would potentially want to acquire that customer for.

105
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:55,520
The bank is going to have a much, much higher LTV than you are going to have on your budgeting app.

106
00:09:56,480 --> 00:10:00,720
You will always lose that battle because they will always be willing to pay more for that customer than you.

107
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:12,400
Again, that was when we looked at and said there's a lot of interesting things in the category, a lot of interesting features that make those products attractive, but we don't think we can effectively compete there in the long term because it is such a big and attractive market.

108
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:17,520
Because the LTBs can be so high if you actually provide the financial services, which is not something we're ever going to do.

109
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:19,360
Yeah, no, that makes a ton of sense.

110
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:20,400
And then you had a follow up.

111
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:23,680
I kind of interrupted you, but I appreciate the kind of concrete examples.

112
00:10:24,080 --> 00:10:30,080
And I think again, super helpful for folks to think about as they're thinking about growing their business in those same competitive dynamics.

113
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:42,640
I mean, you know, I know folks in the meditation space and the language learning space, and it's like, yeah, those are big spaces, but it's important to understand those competitive dynamics and understand the challenges you're going to face along the way.

114
00:10:42,960 --> 00:10:44,240
But yeah, you had more to say.

115
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:54,080
One point on that, I'd say, is in those spaces, I think there is an argument for if you''re bringing something truly new and innovative to the table, the whole thing, like, are you ten X better than the current solutions?

116
00:10:54,640 --> 00:11:04,800
Not to say you shouldn't try, but that just being ten X better doesn't solve all of those dynamics, meaning like you still have to get your product in the hands of users and let them see that you're ten X better.

117
00:11:05,120 --> 00:11:11,600
So you still need to have a strategy for how you're going to do that user acquisition if everyone's already doing paid and they're already gobbling off that top of the funnel.

118
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:20,480
And then the third lens I was going to add is we thought a lot about, are we tapping into existing demand or do we have to go out and build a new product category?

119
00:11:20,960 --> 00:11:22,960
Not to say one is always going to be better than the other.

120
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:24,560
They're just different and they have different.

121
00:11:25,680 --> 00:11:26,480
capital requirements.

122
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:44,720
The way we thought about it was, if you're tapping into existing demand, you're probably going to have more competition upfront, but it's typically a lower capital investment because you can start doing things like performance marketing right away or ASO right away and start getting users in in a relatively profitable or rational way.

123
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:48,560
If you have to go out and build a new category, people don't know this product exists.

124
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:49,520
You're the first to market.

125
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:51,280
You have to build demand for it.

126
00:11:51,840 --> 00:11:55,520
You're probably running at a loss for some period of time while you go out and do that.

127
00:11:55,760 --> 00:11:58,640
And so you need to factor that into your thinking of, can I do that?

128
00:11:58,800 --> 00:11:59,600
And am I willing to do that?

129
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:07,440
So I think particularly for independent app developers, one thing I talk to developers a lot about is like, that's a really great idea and that's really exciting.

130
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:12,560
Like, do you have the resources to see it through and scale it on the other side of that though?

131
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:14,240
I think that's how you should think about it.

132
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:14,720
Yeah.

133
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:16,480
And then how did you apply that thinking?

134
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:21,120
How do you today apply that thinking when it comes to building new features?

135
00:12:21,360 --> 00:12:24,880
I mean, this is something where folks maybe aren't always strategic enough.

136
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:27,440
It's like, oh, we'll build this new feature because it's cool.

137
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:34,240
But like then, I imagine there's some thought that should go into, is this a secondary product market fit?

138
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:36,160
Is there a keyword that this feature?

139
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:54,960
is going to allow us to compete on that we're not competing on currently or like what was the kind of strategic thinking behind expanding feature sets and secondary product market fit and then even where like where maybe spinning up a new app was a better solution than kind of adding features to an existing app.

140
00:12:55,520 --> 00:12:55,600
Yeah.

141
00:12:55,840 --> 00:12:57,280
So I think you hit on all the main points.

142
00:12:57,440 --> 00:13:12,640
The one thing I would say at the front is the biggest mistake I see to your point on building features because they're cool is teams build what they think should be in the product and what they think would be cool to be in the product and at no point do they talk to the users and try and understand what your users want?

143
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:14,800
And are you building what they want and need?

144
00:13:15,200 --> 00:13:18,240
It's really easy to just keep building what you think is cool.

145
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:23,280
And then those features never get used and they get lost in the shuffle and you end up with kind of a crowded product on the other side of that.

146
00:13:23,520 --> 00:13:27,280
So strongly recommend, like, talk to your users, get feedback from them around what they want.

147
00:13:27,440 --> 00:13:28,960
Because ultimately, that's what matters.

148
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:31,840
You're not building a product for yourself, you're building a product for other people.

149
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:37,680
Within that, though, we did think about it in terms of, so when we're building a new feature, is it a feature or a product?

150
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:47,360
My general bias in the mobile space is towards slimmer products and being okay, having multiple products that serve adjacent use cases.

151
00:13:48,160 --> 00:13:53,520
I think the mobile form factor has a lot of benefits, but it also has some limitations.

152
00:13:53,840 --> 00:14:00,560
And I think having a product that, for example, has like eight different features on a smartphone app and like a six-inch screen.

153
00:14:01,360 --> 00:14:06,480
It's very hard to get users to understand all of those features and effectively switch between them and use them.

154
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:13,760
And I think maybe having two or three products that have two or three features each could actually deliver more value.

155
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:19,920
And you may even get more financial value out of it because you could better segment users into the product that actually suits their needs.

156
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:21,200
So we did think a lot about that.

157
00:14:21,280 --> 00:14:27,760
And we generally aired on the side of less features and being more willing to just build a net new product if we thought there was demand there.

158
00:14:28,160 --> 00:14:32,640
And then when we were actually doing features, we went through that analysis and said, this should be a feature in the product.

159
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:37,600
Two ways we thought about it there, which is, are we trying to increase ARPU or are we trying to increase retention?

160
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:40,800
And I would say all of your features should fit into one of those two buckets.

161
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:45,760
So I'm not sure what the other rationale for releasing a new feature is if you're not going to increase ARPU or retention.

162
00:14:46,160 --> 00:14:47,120
That's a great lens.

163
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:50,320
So then you have to think about, like, what am I really trying to achieve with that?

164
00:14:50,480 --> 00:14:52,800
So we had some features where we released and it was an upsell.

165
00:14:53,040 --> 00:14:56,640
And we said, this is, you know, an adjacent use case that helps solve the same problem.

166
00:14:56,960 --> 00:15:02,160
One example is in a Robo Killer, we integrated data broker removal service and that was an upsell.

167
00:15:02,240 --> 00:15:05,920
So we sold you into the base subscription and then we said, do you also want this on the other side?

168
00:15:06,240 --> 00:15:11,280
It was really successful, a really good way to increase ARPU and it made sense having it as a feature within the product.

169
00:15:11,760 --> 00:15:27,680
There are other things you're going to release or features you're going to release where it's more about retention and engagement and I think the strategies for how you develop those products or those features, sorry, and how you market those to your user bases are very different.

170
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:32,640
So having that clarity upfront, is this a retention or an ARPU oriented feature?

171
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:42,160
I do think it's very important in thinking about that kind of market sizing both for features that you're adding on to and new apps or new lines of business.

172
00:15:43,120 --> 00:15:51,600
One of the things I know comes up a lot in the industry is how many apps seem to get stuck in this like 10 to 30 million a year revenue threshold.

173
00:15:52,240 --> 00:16:00,800
How did you think about hitting that ceiling, like why some apps hit the ceiling and then why some apps don't and how to push through that?

174
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:02,240
Maybe not the answer.

175
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:03,520
Everyone's going to want to hear here.

176
00:16:04,240 --> 00:16:09,680
I tend to think most apps will hit that ceiling and it's actually very hard to break through it.

177
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:18,880
So the way we tended to think about it and the way I still think about it in the way I still think about it today is more of accepting that as a constraint for the mobile business.

178
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:23,680
And then how do you architect around it or how do you build a business given that constraint?

179
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:26,800
And I think there's a couple of reasons why that's just a real constraint.

180
00:16:27,200 --> 00:16:36,960
One is if you think about like user acquisition at a very basic level, you're going to get organic users, people just coming to the app store searching for things and they find your app as a result of it and download it.

181
00:16:37,120 --> 00:16:41,840
And then you're going to do paid user acquisition and go out and bring users pull users into your app.

182
00:16:42,320 --> 00:16:48,560
On the organic side, it's hard for any individual developer to materially move the amount of searches happening.

183
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:49,440
in the App Store.

184
00:16:49,840 --> 00:16:52,320
That's a pretty static figure that's relatively out of your control.

185
00:16:52,960 --> 00:16:56,000
So all you can do is increase your ranking for those keywords.

186
00:16:56,160 --> 00:17:04,960
And at some point, you're going to hit your enough top three rankings, you're going to get your rankings and they're going to stop being able to move and you're going to almost like maximize your share of that organic pie.

187
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:11,520
Now, not everyone always hits that point, but like there's a cap on the organic side for most products.

188
00:17:11,920 --> 00:17:13,120
And then you get to the paid side.

189
00:17:13,280 --> 00:17:21,840
And I think the challenge there is most people similarly hit a ceiling on the paid side because mobile LTVs just aren't in the grand scheme of things.

190
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,160
If you go to other markets like B2B SaaS.

191
00:17:24,640 --> 00:17:26,400
LTVs and mobile are pretty low.

192
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:29,920
Most people are operating under $100 subscriber LTV.

193
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:33,360
So there's only so much you can then pay to acquire a subscriber.

194
00:17:33,920 --> 00:17:42,000
And then basically what happens is you're going to keep spending and at some point your CAC is going to reach that maximum and you can't spend more unpaid without going unprofitable.

195
00:17:42,160 --> 00:17:43,360
So you decide to stop spending.

196
00:17:43,680 --> 00:17:55,120
And then when those two things happen, that's when you kind of hit a ceiling on your growth and you can get, you know, call it single digit growth on the other side of that through LTV improvements, testing conversion rate improvements, things like that.

197
00:17:55,520 --> 00:17:58,000
But you're not going to be doubling in size on the other side of that.

198
00:17:58,480 --> 00:18:02,880
I think that's a very real constraint for a lot of apps and a lot of mobile first products.

199
00:18:03,360 --> 00:18:08,000
Though again, the way we think about it is that's why you see a lot of portfolios where it's okay.

200
00:18:08,640 --> 00:18:12,400
If you accept that as a constraint, the other way to grow is to have multiple products.

201
00:18:12,640 --> 00:18:21,520
And if you have three $30 million products, that's just potentially as good and maybe even better because of the diversification benefit as having one single $90 million revenue product.

202
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:23,920
So I do think it's a real constraint.

203
00:18:24,080 --> 00:18:27,600
I don't think it's a constraint on the ability to build great mobile businesses though.

204
00:18:28,080 --> 00:18:30,640
And I actually think it could be an opportunity if thought about the right way.

205
00:18:31,200 --> 00:18:42,640
Yeah, but it's an important thing to think about, especially for those who've raised funding and kind of increased the bar of success is that to understand this eventuality is important.

206
00:18:43,040 --> 00:19:26,560
And one of the more interesting ideas on that paid front that I've heard Eric Sue for gosh probably like six or eight years ago talked about how this idea that if there is ultimately kind of a limited ideal customer profile for your app and a limited pool of people, especially willing to pay whatever, you know, higher price, which a lot of apps are kind of tending toward now, there is a limited pool of people who care enough to subscribe to an app that does XYZ and the more you spend and the more you're willing to stretch your cack, the faster you saturate that pool and you still end up at that same eventuality.

207
00:19:26,800 --> 00:19:33,840
So if today you're growing at 200% and like things, it just feels like you can't spend enough and you're just going so fast.

208
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:48,400
You may just be rushing faster toward that same cliff, not necessarily kind of escaping the laws of thermodynamics and the speed at which you're currently going isn't necessarily sustainable just because.

209
00:19:48,400 --> 00:19:55,760
Just because you seem to be accelerating faster than other apps, as you've said, and I'll reiterate, like, I don't think this is universally true.

210
00:19:56,080 --> 00:20:14,720
There will be breakout products like a Duolingo, but it's like those are the exceptions that prove the rule that there are just so many spaces that don't have a big enough TAM, don't have enough kind of willing consumers to pay these higher prices that make the return on ad spend work, that you're going to find some ceiling.

211
00:20:14,880 --> 00:20:18,960
And for some apps, it actually may be lower than that $10 to $30 million threshold.

212
00:20:19,440 --> 00:20:24,800
For some apps, it may be a little higher, and then you're going to have those that break through, but kind of understanding those dynamics, I think, is super important.

213
00:20:25,680 --> 00:20:36,880
in kind of informing strategy, how much you're willing to spend, how much you're willing to stretch out your payback periods, how fast you're willing to invest that revenue and things along those lines.

214
00:20:37,200 --> 00:20:45,200
And I actually think on that point, one thing to think through is if you actually think about a lot of those exceptions, like a Duolingo, even a Spotify is an inherently mobile first product.

215
00:20:45,760 --> 00:20:52,880
A common thread I see at least is they have big free user bases, or at least for a large part of their life had very big free user bases.

216
00:20:53,360 --> 00:20:58,880
I say this as someone who has always prioritized limited free usage or free user bases.

217
00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:08,960
I'm in favor of even like hard paywalls in some examples, but you have to also accept when you're doing that, you are playing into the, I'm probably capping this, 10, 20, 30 million of revenue.

218
00:21:09,360 --> 00:21:20,640
There are not very many paid only subscription products outside of the streaming world that really gain traction without that substantial free user base that drives adoption and community and brand.

219
00:21:21,120 --> 00:21:22,560
Yeah, that's a really good point.

220
00:21:23,120 --> 00:21:25,280
How do you think retention plays into all this?

221
00:21:25,520 --> 00:21:36,000
Because in for an app that does actually have ridiculously good retention, you can still build a great business layering those cohorts year after year after year.

222
00:21:36,720 --> 00:21:50,080
where even if you kind of max out the number of users you can acquire, if you're actually retaining a much higher percentage, you can still continue growing at high rates, but that's a challenge.

223
00:21:50,720 --> 00:21:50,800
Yeah.

224
00:21:51,360 --> 00:21:52,640
I do think that's the way forward.

225
00:21:52,800 --> 00:22:02,480
And I think that's what you should be focused on is like one of your most important signals in your business is how your subscribers retain and what's that average life cycle and you want it to be higher and longer as much as possible.

226
00:22:03,120 --> 00:22:07,600
But even there, I think there's two challenges coming back to why things cap out.

227
00:22:07,920 --> 00:22:09,040
One, it comes back to LTV.

228
00:22:09,360 --> 00:22:24,000
Your attention's a very obviously an important input to your LTV and in the mobile app space, like great retention is often like on a blended basis across all your durations, fifty percent of subscribers every year is like you're doing pretty well if you're doing that.

229
00:22:24,160 --> 00:22:28,240
But that also means fifty percent of your cohort is leaving at the end of that year.

230
00:22:28,640 --> 00:22:36,240
So that's one kind of impact your LTV, like if you think about B2B SaaS, they're at like, you know, sometimes over 100 percent retention because they're actually upselling.

231
00:22:36,720 --> 00:22:39,600
That's a very different world they're living in from an LTV perspective.

232
00:22:40,160 --> 00:23:16,480
And then two, like again, if your incoming new users is flat, so your cohort size is flat and you're churning out half of the cohorts every year like you can only grow so much now like higher retention is better these are still recurring businesses and as you get into years like two three four five for a lot of these cohorts it's actually much higher than 50 percent retention is what you're seeing so you definitely get a stacking benefit as you drag these things out i do think you one way to optimize that is to your earlier point is like don't blow the doors off marketing right away and like have one giant cohort that's going to slowly attract over time.

233
00:23:17,200 --> 00:23:33,360
Be patient and bring in more evenly sized cohorts over a multi-year period and you can actually potentially get much better recurring revenue and like a much more evenly spaced out renewal base across various points on that renewal curve.

234
00:23:33,840 --> 00:23:39,600
So you can get really sticky bases of revenue that are good relative sizes to those new users that are coming in.

235
00:23:40,320 --> 00:23:56,320
And then part of this whole topic around like hitting these caps and the reason folks think about these things is that especially if you've taken any amount of funding, but even if you haven't, I think a lot of people are thinking about the eventuality of a potential exit.

236
00:23:56,720 --> 00:24:00,160
So then how a potential exit comes into play.

237
00:24:01,440 --> 00:24:10,320
With these challenges in scale, what do you think the range of potential outcomes exists for subscription apps today?

238
00:24:11,040 --> 00:24:13,680
So the outliers like Duolingo, you can IPO.

239
00:24:14,320 --> 00:24:21,040
That's a that's a great strategy if you can execute on it and setting that one aside because I do think that's the exception, not the norm.

240
00:24:21,520 --> 00:24:26,080
Typically what we'd see is there's three buyers of these assets if you want an exit.

241
00:24:26,960 --> 00:24:33,360
One is you could sell to there are many portfolio players in this space, people who are rolling up diversified portfolios of apps.

242
00:24:33,840 --> 00:24:35,680
They're always willing to take a look at things.

243
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:39,120
I think there are very very willing seller, probably your most realistic seller.

244
00:24:39,520 --> 00:24:41,360
Their business is buying and selling apps.

245
00:24:41,440 --> 00:24:51,280
So they're probably going to be more regimented on valuation than some of your other buyers because like their whole thing is they have to get good ROI in every single deal for their business model to work.

246
00:24:51,520 --> 00:24:53,920
So they're going to be pretty strict on their valuations.

247
00:24:54,320 --> 00:24:56,080
Second is you can sell to private equity.

248
00:24:56,400 --> 00:24:59,520
There are some small scale private equity players who are interested in this space.

249
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:03,200
I think the key there though is it's hard to sell them a single app.

250
00:25:03,600 --> 00:25:07,440
So if you're just a single app developer, you're probably looking at the app roll up portfolios.

251
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:16,400
If you've built a portfolio of apps and you can be able to hand off almost more of a business than a product to those private equity buyers.

252
00:25:16,480 --> 00:25:20,560
And you can say, hey, I have these three apps and I have a consolidated tech stack behind it.

253
00:25:20,800 --> 00:25:24,640
And this is a great platform that you can go out and add more products to.

254
00:25:25,520 --> 00:25:32,320
That's a story and like a narrative that can get you into the kind of lower end of private equity and then get an exit there.

255
00:25:32,560 --> 00:25:35,360
And then lastly, for certain apps, I think strategic buyers are very real.

256
00:25:35,600 --> 00:25:41,040
Like you saw Under Armour, what was that five, ten years ago at this point, did a bunch of acquisitions in this space.

257
00:25:41,440 --> 00:25:47,360
I think you can see similar things where there's a strategic who has a strong rationale for wanting to buy your product.

258
00:25:47,840 --> 00:25:49,520
That's probably for any given product.

259
00:25:49,840 --> 00:26:00,880
There's going to be a handful of companies that you'll know will make sense for that, but you can have an eye towards trying to establish relationships or making yourself visible to them to set up that a potential exit.

260
00:26:01,040 --> 00:26:06,480
And then the last one I always advise people is also like, I don't think everyone has to exit per se.

261
00:26:07,120 --> 00:26:09,600
Again, part of the benefit is these are recurring businesses.

262
00:26:09,760 --> 00:26:11,680
You can build up a sticky renewal base.

263
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:28,160
And maybe the right thing for you is to actually just at some point shift a little bit more into maintenance mode where if what you're trying to do is spend more time on other things, you do that, but you still take your cash flow from maintaining a product and continuing to provide the value that those users thought they were getting when they purchased the product.

264
00:26:28,560 --> 00:26:34,240
I mean, I think like in the mobile app space, you could do that with a reasonable time commitment that still allows you to go do other things.

265
00:26:34,800 --> 00:26:37,360
Yeah, I wanted to step back to the strategic buyer.

266
00:26:37,760 --> 00:26:41,280
It seems like we saw a lot more of this early on.

267
00:26:42,320 --> 00:27:00,480
I don't feel like I've seen as much of this recently, although maybe I'm just not paying enough attention to the MA space, but have you seen any kind of strategic acquisitions that you think are interesting or do you see specific categories and companies that might make sense for these kind of strategic purchases?

268
00:27:01,200 --> 00:27:02,400
Yeah, I do think you can.

269
00:27:02,640 --> 00:27:05,040
So like actually the budgeting space is one example.

270
00:27:05,440 --> 00:27:09,280
Rocket Mortgage bought, I think was Truebill at the time, and now it's Rocket Money.

271
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:13,840
Because for them, that's actually to the reason why we never found the category attractive.

272
00:27:14,080 --> 00:27:20,880
It's a great customer acquisition funnel for them to get people into their budgeting tool and then sell them actual financial products on the other side of that.

273
00:27:21,280 --> 00:27:24,240
So I think financial services is definitely one where you could continue to see that.

274
00:27:24,560 --> 00:27:38,480
I think privacy security is another area where you have some big consumer brands like the Nortons of the world and the McAfees and things like that, where they could still be interested in maybe increasing their mobile presence or their mobile brand.

275
00:27:39,360 --> 00:27:41,040
Those are the two big ones that stand out to me.

276
00:27:41,200 --> 00:27:45,920
And I also think health and wellness, although to your point, I think that's one where we've seen that fade recently.

277
00:27:46,480 --> 00:27:57,760
And I generally agree with your classification here, which is if you look back like 10 or 15 years, mobile was this like exciting new platform and was like desktop versus mobile.

278
00:27:57,920 --> 00:28:04,080
I think we're kind of on the other side of that where mobile is more of a standard and just kind of baked into everything everyone does.

279
00:28:04,720 --> 00:28:07,600
And most people have some degree of mobile and desktop presence.

280
00:28:07,920 --> 00:28:16,560
So I do think it's you had a rush early on where it was like every big company felt like they had to have a mobile play and they were using MA as a way to accomplish that.

281
00:28:17,040 --> 00:28:24,480
And now I think it actually is more tightly tied to product fit and like does this product make sense?

282
00:28:24,640 --> 00:28:25,840
Does it generate us more revenue?

283
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:32,880
Does it help lower our customer acquisition costs, not just like mobile for the sake of mobile, which you got more of in, you know, 15 years ago.

284
00:28:33,360 --> 00:28:33,440
Yeah.

285
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:48,800
And in two recent acquisitions that I think point to that is both Under Armour and the Weather Channel selling to Francisco Partners, a private equity firm, where both of those I would have guessed, you know, had you asked me.

286
00:28:49,520 --> 00:28:52,160
three years ago who would buy something like that?

287
00:28:52,320 --> 00:28:55,280
I mean, famously, IBM actually bought the Weather Channel.

288
00:28:55,680 --> 00:29:00,320
And from what I understand, they still maintain the B2B side of the weather business.

289
00:29:01,200 --> 00:29:04,800
And then Under Armour having bought My Fitness Pal and a few others.

290
00:29:05,360 --> 00:29:09,520
And a few others, and then they spun it out and now it's landed at Private Equity.

291
00:29:09,840 --> 00:29:14,320
It's like My Fitness Pal kind of being a darling of that health and wellness space.

292
00:29:14,880 --> 00:29:22,960
It's like if someone was going to get a strategic acquisition by a bigger player, that's one that I would have guessed would, and they didn't.

293
00:29:23,360 --> 00:29:30,160
So those are, I think think, two data points showing, yeah, maybe the strategic acquisition space is limited.

294
00:29:30,560 --> 00:29:35,680
But then one other data point on the other side is, you know, Ziff Davis acquired Lose It recently.

295
00:29:36,160 --> 00:29:37,680
That is in the health and wellness space.

296
00:29:37,920 --> 00:29:39,520
They're a big media conglomerate.

297
00:29:39,920 --> 00:29:45,920
So it's like there are still opportunities, but it's just, yeah, it seems very different in 2024 than it has been.

298
00:29:46,320 --> 00:29:48,080
Yeah, it's going to be a very small universe.

299
00:29:48,400 --> 00:30:02,800
Typically, when we would do, I also worked on sales out of our portfolio at IEC, when we'd make buyer lists for most companies, your strategic buyers are like five to ten potential, and then you have a list of like a hundred financial buyers who could potentially be interested in it.

300
00:30:03,120 --> 00:30:09,840
And I think that's going to stay true here where it's a very small population of potential strategics and then a much larger population of more financially motivated buyers.

301
00:30:10,320 --> 00:30:10,400
Yeah.

302
00:30:10,880 --> 00:30:21,760
I think few people listening to this podcast don't have some inkling in the back of their minds, like an acquisition or some kind of bigger liquidity event would be an interesting prospect in the future.

303
00:30:22,160 --> 00:30:25,520
And so kind of understanding those dynamics, I think is important.

304
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:34,320
The other thing I'd advise is if that is what you're optimizing for, if that's what you seriously want the end goal to be, build towards that goal.

305
00:30:34,640 --> 00:30:35,920
Don't just assume it's going to happen.

306
00:30:36,240 --> 00:31:14,640
in like be thoughtful around who is a potential buyer what can I do to make this more interesting to that buyer or even like if you haven't built a thing yet and you want to build a thing to get acquired like have that be part of your selection criteria of what you're going to build is what does the acquisition pool look like for this thing and therefore do I want to build it or not that both from a product perspective and then I'd also say from an operational perspective the cleaner and more professionalized your setup is the easier it's going to be to find a buyer for that buyers don't love messiness they're already taking on a lot of risk by doing an upfront payment for an uncertain outcome on the other side anything you could do to reduce reduce that messiness for them is great.

307
00:31:14,960 --> 00:31:15,040
Yeah.

308
00:31:15,520 --> 00:31:21,440
Any other advice on kind of opening up those potential doors, strategic or PE?

309
00:31:21,760 --> 00:31:46,400
I mean, imagine like if you're building toward a private equity exit and you think that's kind of the maximum, the optimal return, then building something that's throwing off cash and being more careful, more disciplined with spend, being more disciplined with expenses, like showing that profitability or the opportunity for profitability is something that is going to be way more valued by private equity.

310
00:31:47,040 --> 00:31:54,480
Again, maybe you shouldn't be be building exclusively this direction because we know it's such a limited pool of potential outcomes.

311
00:31:54,880 --> 00:32:07,200
But if you are hoping for a strategic acquisition, would you then maybe be trying to build a more of a brand or have a unique take or any advice on that spectrum of building toward a hopeful exit?

312
00:32:07,840 --> 00:32:20,320
Yeah, I do think particularly if you're going to be going to a financial buyer, having a track record that they can understand and project out and say, if I just do more of that, this is going to go well for me is important.

313
00:32:20,640 --> 00:32:24,640
So for example, you've been running unprofitably your entire life and then you go to them.

314
00:32:25,040 --> 00:32:27,120
and say, but if you buy this, it's going to become profitable.

315
00:32:27,520 --> 00:32:36,480
Like that's just a harder hill for them to get over as opposed to saying, look, it's already been profitable for the last three years, just keep doing X, Y, Z, and this is going to go well for you.

316
00:32:36,800 --> 00:32:38,480
That's a much easier narrative to sell them.

317
00:32:38,720 --> 00:32:49,920
So I do think being to a point where you're either already profitable or your unit economics are clearly going to drive profitability in the future, even if like right now, it's not immediately profitable.

318
00:32:50,560 --> 00:32:52,080
That is great for the financial buyers.

319
00:32:52,560 --> 00:32:54,720
It does probably matter a bit less for the strategic buyers.

320
00:32:54,880 --> 00:33:00,000
And I do think like brand and maybe even just like the size of your user base matters more for strategics.

321
00:33:00,560 --> 00:33:07,360
I think, for example, strategics will always place more value on free users and community than a financial buyer will.

322
00:33:07,840 --> 00:33:11,360
So there are differences there in terms of what they're going to value.

323
00:33:11,600 --> 00:33:12,800
Yeah, well, this has been fascinating.

324
00:33:12,960 --> 00:33:29,200
I mean, picking your brain, having been in this space for so long and looking at so many apps over such a long period of time, both, I didn't even think about that you would be working on selling parts of IAC's portfolio while also potentially acquiring others.

325
00:33:29,360 --> 00:33:36,960
So you've been both on the buy side and the sell side of this in a really interesting way and across many years of kind of ups and downs.

326
00:33:37,520 --> 00:33:38,000
in the industry.

327
00:33:38,240 --> 00:33:40,560
So yeah, that was really fascinating.

328
00:33:40,800 --> 00:33:42,160
Thanks for sharing all those insights.

329
00:33:42,720 --> 00:33:51,440
So I did want to dive into the more kind of tactical side of things that you learned running as a GM of iTranslate and RoboKiller.

330
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:59,440
The first one there is how to think about user acquisition, LTV, CAC, paybacks.

331
00:33:59,920 --> 00:34:02,000
I mean, it's just such a big hairy mess.

332
00:34:02,160 --> 00:34:05,200
And yeah, I mean, it's actually something we're thinking a lot about at RevenueCat.

333
00:34:05,760 --> 00:34:07,840
We now do have some LTV predictions.

334
00:34:08,240 --> 00:34:12,240
We're kind of teaser going to be adding those to our experiments feature.

335
00:34:13,680 --> 00:34:25,760
And like, we've actually been really careful internally and had a lot of like thought around how we build these products because it's really easy to just throw a PLTV stat out there.

336
00:34:26,240 --> 00:34:28,000
But like, what does it actually mean?

337
00:34:28,320 --> 00:34:28,960
Can I trust it?

338
00:34:29,680 --> 00:34:32,800
Can I buy against it is a really important question.

339
00:34:33,760 --> 00:34:39,040
So, yeah, I'd love to hear kind of your thoughts around how to think about these metrics.

340
00:34:39,280 --> 00:34:41,440
LTV is hard, particularly if you're in the early days.

341
00:34:41,760 --> 00:34:48,000
What I advise people to do is think more about your range of possible outcomes, because the reality is you have very limited data.

342
00:34:48,800 --> 00:34:55,120
That data is not going to tell you a perfect story on the other side of it and say with 100% confidence, this cohort's LTV is going to be X.

343
00:34:55,680 --> 00:34:57,440
You're getting a directional estimate at best.

344
00:34:57,840 --> 00:35:08,480
So the way we do it in the early days of a product and what I advise people we work with to do is run a range of scenarios and say, you know, plus or minus twenty percent and maybe five percent increments.

345
00:35:08,640 --> 00:35:10,080
What are my possible LTVs?

346
00:35:10,480 --> 00:35:11,840
Assign some probabilities to them.

347
00:35:12,080 --> 00:35:13,360
Come up with your best estimate.

348
00:35:13,920 --> 00:35:15,360
Start making decisions and acting on that.

349
00:35:15,520 --> 00:35:27,680
And then just go back and check it every month or two months as you're getting that retention data in, start validating what you could hopefully start doing is if you start with, hey, I have six possible LTVs, then I'm giving some weighting to each of them.

350
00:35:28,160 --> 00:35:36,480
Then you can get it down to four LTVs over time and then two and then three or, you know, however that goes and eventually get to a more precise LTV estimate that you're going to have.

351
00:35:36,800 --> 00:35:47,760
But like you're going to need maturity just from a scale perspective to get to a relatively precise LTV, like you need multiple years of data realistically to be able to reliably predict that.

352
00:35:48,080 --> 00:35:49,600
So that's okay in my mind.

353
00:35:49,840 --> 00:35:52,640
You just have to, again, like know where you're at in that cycle.

354
00:35:52,800 --> 00:36:00,720
Are you in the less certain and therefore you need to be more flexible and dynamic or are you in the more certain side of your cycle where you're more mature and you can kind of have.

355
00:36:01,120 --> 00:36:03,280
a more of a singular target of how you think about things.

356
00:36:03,600 --> 00:36:10,640
And maybe this is one of those things you need to kind of pick a point in time and say, what's my LTV going to be at one year?

357
00:36:10,880 --> 00:36:12,480
What's my LTV going to be at two years?

358
00:36:12,880 --> 00:36:25,200
And kind of more cash flow prediction because LTV is such a loaded term that I don't even like in the subscription app industry in that I've seen with my own app.

359
00:36:25,360 --> 00:36:30,880
I launched it for my weather, my dinky little side project weather app launched it seven years ago.

360
00:36:31,600 --> 00:37:14,800
And still to this day, I've retained like ten, fifteen percent of the original cohort who who bought it seven years ago and so trying to estimate that into the future and then saying my seven year LTV is going to be x you can't actually calculate the average lifetime value of a user because it can go decades into the future i mean there's products like netflix i subscribed to netflix as a dvd customer in 2000 what 4 or something in 2001 and then i transitioned from dvds to their streaming service and i've been a monthly paid customer for close to 20 years now i think that's where the rest of the industry is going.

361
00:37:15,120 --> 00:37:26,080
Like a weather app, whether it's on AR glasses, ten years from now, you know, I'm probably going to have users that have twenty year lifetime, thirty year lifetime.

362
00:37:26,480 --> 00:37:46,160
Even my simple little dinky side project weather app, I'm ranting here, but it's like, I don't like LTV as a term because nobody really, you can't actually estimate an average lifetime value of a user, but you need some metric to buy against for cash flow.

363
00:37:46,320 --> 00:38:01,120
And that's where actually, you know, this is something Dan., he is a colleague of mine at RevenueCat, formerly a colleague of yours at Teltec at Rubberkiller, and he brought to our charts this metric called Realized LTV.

364
00:38:01,360 --> 00:38:12,960
The way I think about it is like day X Realized LTV because in the RevenueCat charts you can actually pick, like, how much revenue have I made on this cohort at 30 days, at 90 days, at a year?

365
00:38:13,760 --> 00:38:16,160
And there's a little dropdown that you can select it.

366
00:38:16,400 --> 00:38:24,640
And to me, that's just such a more helpful way to think about, quote unquote, lifetime value is more like.

367
00:38:25,120 --> 00:38:45,040
picking different time points and doing those calculations toward like what's the range of expected revenue at twelve months, at twenty four months, at thirty six months, because it just feels like that's so much more helpful than thinking of it as this is all the money I'm ever going to make from this cohort, and here's the line I'm drawing in the sand.

368
00:38:45,520 --> 00:38:46,160
Totally agree.

369
00:38:46,400 --> 00:38:47,200
A couple of thoughts there.

370
00:38:47,600 --> 00:38:49,760
One, we always did cap or LTVs.

371
00:38:49,920 --> 00:38:59,600
So to your point, maybe calling them lifetimes is not actually a accurate way of framing that, but we typically would cap them at a certain number of years for two reasons.

372
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:03,360
One is like you have to get paid back at some point.

373
00:39:03,600 --> 00:39:05,280
So like you have to care about your payback period.

374
00:39:05,440 --> 00:39:09,920
If you're buying against a 20 year LTV, maybe you're not even making a profit to your 15.

375
00:39:10,000 --> 00:39:13,280
And like that's not a super functional business model to be managing.

376
00:39:13,520 --> 00:39:16,160
And then two is not to get like two finance nerd on people.

377
00:39:16,480 --> 00:39:17,600
There's a time value of money.

378
00:39:18,080 --> 00:39:21,200
So like a dollar in 20 years is worth less than a dollar today.

379
00:39:21,600 --> 00:39:30,240
So even if you're still having some of your cohort renewing and paying you in 20 years, if you discount that back to what is that actually worth to me today, it may be very minimal.

380
00:39:30,640 --> 00:39:32,960
And therefore, not actually worth baking into your LTV.

381
00:39:33,040 --> 00:39:33,680
So we did cap it.

382
00:39:33,840 --> 00:39:34,880
I would advise people to cap it.

383
00:39:35,040 --> 00:39:40,560
And also to your point, I think coming back to like where you're at in your maturity life cycle, you also need to think about payback periods.

384
00:39:40,640 --> 00:39:48,320
So like typically what we say is like when you're earlier in your life cycle, you can't be waiting twelve months to make a profit on a cohort.

385
00:39:48,560 --> 00:40:06,640
You need to have tighter payback windows because you just don't have a lot of cash at hand to spend when you're later in your life and you have more money in the bank, you could potentially think about, you know, if maybe you started a two year LTV, maybe you start expanding that to a three or a four year LTV and being okay having a twelve plus month payback period.

386
00:40:07,360 --> 00:40:12,640
Again, I think you can play with those levers as time goes on, but I would definitely advise capping.

387
00:40:13,120 --> 00:40:13,600
your LTV.

388
00:40:14,000 --> 00:40:15,920
It just will make your life simpler and easier.

389
00:40:16,080 --> 00:40:20,240
And it's at worst, you're being conservative and like that's not a terrible thing to do.

390
00:40:20,560 --> 00:40:26,960
And then I would think about payback periods as almost like a separate and distinct metric from your LTV to CAC or your ROAS.

391
00:40:27,520 --> 00:40:29,200
But then how does that work out practically?

392
00:40:29,600 --> 00:40:33,680
Like in what time frames would you cap your LTV?

393
00:40:33,920 --> 00:40:40,800
Like, would you look at a particular app and say, based on the cash flow of this app, we need ROAS at day 90.

394
00:40:41,120 --> 00:40:45,040
And so we're going to predict, I mean, would you call that a predicted?

395
00:40:45,840 --> 00:40:53,840
Again, it's like you're not predicting lif cash flow within ninety days, not an actual lifetime.

396
00:40:54,240 --> 00:40:56,400
How did you do those specific kind of projections?

397
00:40:56,640 --> 00:41:01,600
Like what's our cash flow going to be at nine months, ninety days, at nine months, at twelve months?

398
00:41:02,000 --> 00:41:03,680
How did you think about that tactically?

399
00:41:04,080 --> 00:41:08,320
Yeah, so we did have an in-house proprietary system for forecasting LTVs.

400
00:41:08,960 --> 00:41:10,640
Again, we benefited from being more mature.

401
00:41:10,800 --> 00:41:22,640
So we had a ton of data on all of our products where if we got, you know, an initial renewal point or initial two renewal points from a given cohort, we could pretty reliably predict out then what the next twenty would look from there.

402
00:41:23,040 --> 00:41:28,960
And also what you see is typically in those first couple of renewals, actually where you see the bulk of your churn, and then it starts flatlining after that.

403
00:41:29,600 --> 00:41:31,280
We were able to pretty reliably predict those.

404
00:41:31,680 --> 00:41:34,320
Then we manage on LTV to CAC predominantly.

405
00:41:34,880 --> 00:41:36,320
I think of them as two separate ways.

406
00:41:36,720 --> 00:41:39,520
I think you either have a primary filter of what's my payback period.

407
00:41:40,000 --> 00:41:46,880
Basically, I'm at a stage in my company's life cycle where I need to make my money back either in the first month or in the first three months.

408
00:41:47,360 --> 00:41:49,920
Therefore, that's the primary thing I'm optimizing for.

409
00:41:50,640 --> 00:41:53,920
Secondarily, I'm looking at LTV to CAC to understand what that looks like.

410
00:41:54,480 --> 00:42:00,160
What you may find is, okay, I need to get paid back the same month, but then what I see is I have a crazy high LTV to CAC.

411
00:42:00,560 --> 00:42:09,200
So once I solve my like cash liquidity problem and I can extend that payback period, I now know that I can spend a lot more money and still have a healthy LTV to CAC.

412
00:42:09,440 --> 00:42:16,160
So then you can think about like what do you need to do from a business perspective to unlock growth, which is find liquidity somehow.

413
00:42:17,840 --> 00:42:24,880
Alternatively, later in life, you're going to be probably more focused on LTV to CAC and say, okay, I have a threshold has to be above the certain LTV to CAC.

414
00:42:25,120 --> 00:42:38,080
And your payback period is almost more of like a sanity check on the side of, okay, if I'm targeting a two or three X LTV to CAC, I want to know how long I'm getting it's taking to get me paid back, but that's probably not the thing that's dr probably not the thing that's driving your business decisions.

415
00:42:38,160 --> 00:42:40,400
It's just more of a nice to know on the side.

416
00:42:40,960 --> 00:42:41,040
Yeah.

417
00:42:41,600 --> 00:42:58,640
Did you have any particular moments where specific new ad spend or even specific new ad creatives or new prices or any specific changes that you saw break your model?

418
00:42:59,200 --> 00:43:04,240
Did you have moments where like, oh, we, you know, ramped up spend on meta and everything looked great.

419
00:43:04,480 --> 00:43:05,600
LTVs were amazing.

420
00:43:05,760 --> 00:43:09,040
And then like you do the three month look back and you're like, oh crap.

421
00:43:09,280 --> 00:43:14,080
Like this cohort sucked.

422
00:43:14,400 --> 00:43:18,080
Like we shouldn't keep acquiring these users in that way.

423
00:43:18,320 --> 00:43:20,080
Do you have any specific examples like that?

424
00:43:20,480 --> 00:43:22,480
A lot is the short answer.

425
00:43:23,040 --> 00:43:24,080
So it's definitely a thing.

426
00:43:24,480 --> 00:43:28,320
A couple of quick examples on price testing that was always a problem.

427
00:43:28,640 --> 00:43:34,080
We generally so we generally skewed either annual or monthly subscriptions or some combination of the two.

428
00:43:34,720 --> 00:43:41,600
You can pretty easily get a sense of what's going to happen to your monthly subscriber retention when you're running a price test if you're willing to wait, you know, a little bit of time.

429
00:43:42,480 --> 00:43:46,640
You're obviously not going to have data on what happens to your annual subscriber retention when you're making that decision.

430
00:43:46,720 --> 00:43:50,960
Like no one's reasonably waiting over a year to make a call on a price test.

431
00:43:51,600 --> 00:43:54,160
So you have to make some simplifying assumptions there.

432
00:43:54,560 --> 00:44:04,000
We typically what we would do is look at the impact on monthly subscriber retention and say in theory the annual retention impact should approximate what we're seeing in monthly subscribers.

433
00:44:04,320 --> 00:44:05,760
That was not always true though.

434
00:44:06,000 --> 00:44:09,760
And in particular, if like one is clearly your primary plan and one is your secondary plan.

435
00:44:09,920 --> 00:44:12,480
So it's like a 70-30 split between those durations.

436
00:44:12,960 --> 00:44:15,040
They won't actually always work the same.

437
00:44:15,680 --> 00:44:20,400
So we often end up with price tests, particularly if there were overweight annual subscriptions.

438
00:44:21,040 --> 00:44:31,360
Sometimes we'd make calls and then you'd see those cohorts come in a year plus later and you'd be like, ah, like that maybe wasn't the right call, unfortunately, or you're very pleasantly surprised and it was a better call than you even anticipated.

439
00:44:31,680 --> 00:44:33,280
I don't have a great solution for avoiding that.

440
00:44:33,440 --> 00:44:43,920
What I typically advise people is you're going to look at your AB test results and just sensibilize it to say, if retention comes in 10% worse, does it change my answer of if I should roll this out or not?

441
00:44:44,000 --> 00:44:46,080
If it comes in 20% worse, does it change my answer?

442
00:44:46,320 --> 00:44:48,560
And then make a decision based on your relative comfort level there.

443
00:44:48,800 --> 00:44:58,640
Like if you have only a 5% buffer on retention before this price test winner is no longer a winner, maybe don't roll that one out or maybe be more cautious about how you roll that one out.

444
00:44:58,880 --> 00:45:03,440
If retention can go down by 50% and that's thing's still a winner, like, close your eyes and roll it out.

445
00:45:03,600 --> 00:45:06,400
You're going to be fine with any reasonable outcomes there.

446
00:45:07,040 --> 00:45:14,080
So I would say, sensitized price tests is a big learning we've had, particularly on your annual subscriptions, and then new marketing channels are definitely a thing.

447
00:45:14,400 --> 00:45:22,880
I think a couple of implications of that are like when you're ramping up on or you're launching up on a new channel, you're ramping up on a new channel, be a little conservative.

448
00:45:23,200 --> 00:45:24,640
Like, don't go full bore.

449
00:45:25,280 --> 00:45:38,960
My experience is actual performance almost always contracts from that initial predicted performance when you're doing a new channel because a combination of retention is probably going to come in a in a little bit worse than you were expecting, because that's just life.

450
00:45:39,440 --> 00:45:45,200
And like those first few users you acquire are almost always the cheapest users you're going to acquire.

451
00:45:45,360 --> 00:45:49,360
And like eventually it's going to start getting more expensive and eventually it's going to start contracting on you.

452
00:45:49,840 --> 00:45:57,200
So like patience is important there and give it a little bit to play out before you just like say, I want to pour gas on the fire.

453
00:45:57,600 --> 00:46:04,000
I do see sometimes people are a little too quick to, in their minds, pour gas on a fire that may not be real.

454
00:46:04,400 --> 00:46:11,680
So I do think you have to think about that, but it's I think just something you have to factor into your decision making and it's just a reality of the world we live in.

455
00:46:11,920 --> 00:46:12,000
Yeah.

456
00:46:12,480 --> 00:46:22,480
How much did you think about the impact and how much did you see the impact of price on those longer retention periods?

457
00:46:22,880 --> 00:46:26,640
So I feel like it's a bit of a trend and I've talked about it a lot in the podcast.

458
00:46:27,040 --> 00:46:39,360
I actually talked about it in the episode just before this one that these much higher subscription prices do work really well to get that payback quickly.

459
00:46:39,520 --> 00:46:48,560
Like this shift toward annual subscriptions and then more specifically the shift toward higher priced annual subscriptions is closing the loop on being able to get that.

460
00:46:49,280 --> 00:46:55,360
like seven day return on ad spend for some apps that seem to be doing really well and finding the right creatives and stuff like that.

461
00:46:56,080 --> 00:47:02,560
But then just inherently, the higher your price, there's going to be some impact on churn.

462
00:47:02,960 --> 00:47:10,960
And how do you think about balancing those things and what did you see in the data as you looked at some of these bigger cohorts in the Mosaic portfolio?

463
00:47:11,600 --> 00:47:14,960
Retention as a general rule decreases as price increases.

464
00:47:15,040 --> 00:47:17,040
So you should think of those as inversely correlated.

465
00:47:17,360 --> 00:47:18,320
Basic logic holds.

466
00:47:18,480 --> 00:47:21,920
If someone's paying more for something, they're more sensitive to the value they're getting out of it.

467
00:47:22,320 --> 00:47:23,680
So we did see that on the whole.

468
00:47:24,480 --> 00:47:31,280
It is definitely not a one-to-one relationship, meaning you're going to net get LTV wins most of the time assuming conversion rates mine up.

469
00:47:31,440 --> 00:47:35,840
So we typically see if you increase price ten percent, maybe retention comes down by a couple percent, for example.

470
00:47:36,560 --> 00:47:38,400
And then it's also not one size fits all.

471
00:47:38,720 --> 00:47:49,360
So some products saw it more than others, and particularly like I always advocate for being really honest with yourself around like what your product does and the value it provides.

472
00:47:50,240 --> 00:47:58,160
I think there are a lot of very successful and very profitable and great products in the mobile space that provide a somewhat thin layer of value.

473
00:47:58,400 --> 00:48:00,800
And I don't say that with any negative.

474
00:48:00,960 --> 00:48:02,240
negativity associated with it.

475
00:48:02,560 --> 00:48:05,520
Like they provide a distinct value, but it's thin.

476
00:48:05,680 --> 00:48:08,000
And like you, they built a great business around that.

477
00:48:08,320 --> 00:48:13,120
But like be realistic about that and know that there's then probably a lower ceiling on how much you can charge for that value.

478
00:48:13,440 --> 00:48:26,400
There are other products that are like Duolingo or language learning is potentially an example there where it's like there's a really deep corpus of content underlined and like you're truly teaching someone a skill, a thing that they pay in the real world, maybe thousands of dollars to go somewhere to learn.

479
00:48:27,040 --> 00:48:28,480
You have more price power there.

480
00:48:28,720 --> 00:48:32,000
And so like be honest with yourself about how much value you're providing.

481
00:48:32,160 --> 00:48:33,600
There aren't necessarily wrong answers there.

482
00:48:33,920 --> 00:48:35,920
The most important thing is just be honest with yourself.

483
00:48:36,240 --> 00:48:36,320
Yeah.

484
00:48:37,360 --> 00:48:52,320
And were there specific leading indicators that you would look at to understand how price, especially on annuals, because again, so much of the industry is shifting towards annual to get those earlier paybacks, but then it lengthens the feedback loop.

485
00:48:52,640 --> 00:49:01,520
Like, were you feeding into the payback model and the retention model and the AB test results, early turning off auto renewal engagement data?

486
00:49:01,840 --> 00:49:09,840
Like, what were the factors that helped you get a window into the future performance when the real data was going to be twelve months out?

487
00:49:10,160 --> 00:49:10,240
Yeah.

488
00:49:10,400 --> 00:49:11,040
So a couple of things.

489
00:49:11,200 --> 00:49:20,240
So, like I said, if you have a shorter term term subscription, like a monthly subscription, seeing the impact on that retention from a similar price increase and being able to extrapolate it out.

490
00:49:20,480 --> 00:49:30,000
Similarly, if you've raised price before and you said, okay, last time I raised an annual price ten percent, this would happen to retention, using that as an indicator of what could happen in the future works.

491
00:49:30,640 --> 00:49:36,560
Also, if you have a free trial, trial to paid conversion is typically going to be a leading indicator of retention on the other side.

492
00:49:36,720 --> 00:49:42,800
So if you're seeing a drop in trial to paid, you're probably going to see a drop in retention, not necessarily one to one again, but it's a leading indicator.

493
00:49:43,360 --> 00:49:48,320
We did not see much success using early cancellation rates as a predictor of churn.

494
00:49:48,880 --> 00:49:50,880
We just didn't, we did a lot of testing around it.

495
00:49:51,200 --> 00:49:53,280
Only a fraction of users actually cancel early.

496
00:49:53,360 --> 00:50:00,080
What you see is you get a lot of cancellations like right around the renewal point when they're kind of getting those emails letting them know that it's coming up.

497
00:50:00,560 --> 00:50:03,200
So we didn't end up using it.

498
00:50:03,840 --> 00:50:06,640
It was not a particularly valuable data point for us.

499
00:50:07,120 --> 00:50:13,680
I think engagement can be a valuable data point if you have the clarity and the tracking and like the good data around those features.

500
00:50:14,080 --> 00:50:24,400
So if you have like a key action the user is going to take in your app or a key feature that they need to use, being able to track that and making sure people are still doing that and still doing that on a recurring basis.

501
00:50:25,040 --> 00:50:27,680
I think can be a good indicator, is definitely a good indicator.

502
00:50:28,080 --> 00:50:32,400
That one gets trickier to feed back into something like an LTV algorithm though.

503
00:50:32,800 --> 00:50:34,640
So that's the, I think, the challenge there.

504
00:50:35,280 --> 00:50:43,760
Often what I see is that it's almost used as like a thing that sits on the side where you have your LTV algorithm and then you're looking at your cohorts and are they doing that action?

505
00:50:43,920 --> 00:50:48,400
And then you're making some in your head, like mental adjustment to what your LTV algorithm is saying.

506
00:50:48,560 --> 00:50:49,920
So it's just hard to feed those back in.

507
00:50:50,160 --> 00:50:51,200
No, that makes a lot of sense.

508
00:50:51,360 --> 00:51:04,960
So it's like if the LTV is saying this, but we see usage retention differing from past cohorts, then I should buy ten percent, then we should discount that LTV ten percent.

509
00:51:05,440 --> 00:51:26,720
I mean, something I've talked about before, like in a in a talk I gave and we have talked about on the podcast, but I loved your insight on, are there specific things that are going to be the most meaningful to track to get that sense, like do you need to pick one specific core user flow, one specific key value that the app delivers?

510
00:51:27,040 --> 00:51:29,920
Is there like, I mean, I guess every app's going to have their own thing.

511
00:51:30,160 --> 00:51:41,520
Any tips on like picking that user action that's going to most strongly correlate with retention, which then will help more strongly correlate with LTV and your financial projections.

512
00:51:42,080 --> 00:51:46,080
Yes, we tried to have one or two max for every product.

513
00:51:46,880 --> 00:51:53,280
I think if you have like seven, then you just you're creating noise and the actual signal is getting lost in that noise.

514
00:51:53,600 --> 00:51:55,840
So really think about like the one or two key things.

515
00:51:56,000 --> 00:52:00,560
So for example, like RoboKiller, it was actually less about what was the user doing.

516
00:52:01,280 --> 00:52:08,160
It was more about are they actually getting spam calls and are we blocking calls on their behalf and trying to understand like the passive usage.

517
00:52:08,720 --> 00:52:16,480
Yeah, like are we providing the value that the user came to us for and that that may not be dependent on an action they've taken.

518
00:52:16,800 --> 00:52:26,320
So that's the lens I would suggest applying is it's more about the is your product providing the value that may be because we had another app called Tap a Call, which is the call recorder.

519
00:52:26,640 --> 00:52:29,200
The value there is recording a call that has to be a user action.

520
00:52:29,280 --> 00:52:31,840
So there it is a user action, did they actually record a call?

521
00:52:32,240 --> 00:52:35,520
But again, the verbal kill example, it's not actually a user action you're looking for.

522
00:52:36,000 --> 00:52:40,320
It's just a thing that happens in their environment and you're confirming that they are getting spam calls.

523
00:52:40,400 --> 00:52:43,600
We are blocking them on their behalf and therefore they're getting value out of it.

524
00:52:43,840 --> 00:52:47,840
And actually even there, like we had two metrics of are they getting spam calls and are we blocking them?

525
00:52:48,080 --> 00:52:50,080
And then also, did we get one wrong?

526
00:52:50,480 --> 00:52:55,840
And for us, that was actually the big flag for us when we needed to do something proactive about retention.

527
00:52:56,080 --> 00:53:00,400
If we saw we got something wrong for a user, that was highly correlated with churn.

528
00:53:00,720 --> 00:53:04,160
So then we needed to remediate that in some way, shape or form.

529
00:53:04,640 --> 00:53:20,720
Probably a good exercise for any app that hasn't already done this is to do some user surveys, do some user interviews, pump all your reviews into ChatGPT and figure out, like, why are people actually paying for the app?

530
00:53:21,040 --> 00:53:22,640
Like, what really is the value?

531
00:53:22,800 --> 00:53:24,720
And I like that one, that RoboKiller.

532
00:53:25,120 --> 00:53:29,440
example is so good is that people are not paying to interact with the app.

533
00:53:29,920 --> 00:53:31,680
They're paying to not interact with the app.

534
00:53:31,920 --> 00:53:36,800
They're paying for the app to sit in the background and block those calls.

535
00:53:37,600 --> 00:53:48,160
At times, an inverse correlation between how much you used RoboKiller and how happy you were with the product, where it's like if you were using it a lot, you actually probably were a less satisfied user because you were trying to do all these like tweaks and coming in to check things.

536
00:53:48,320 --> 00:53:49,040
Did we really get it right?

537
00:53:49,200 --> 00:53:53,120
Like our happiest users kind of forgot we were installed on their phone in a good way.

538
00:53:53,440 --> 00:54:00,640
Even in that case, I mean, this is maybe a rabbit hole to the current conversation, but were there ways in which the user.

539
00:54:01,520 --> 00:54:22,400
did see RoboKiller taking action, like would it pop up a notification like RoboKiller blocked this call or you still have to kind of reinforce the value that you're delivering, like, hey, we blocked fifty calls for you this week alone and reminding people that how much value they're actually getting delivered when it's just sitting there in the background passively delivering value.

540
00:54:22,880 --> 00:54:29,600
It was a challenge for us for a couple of technical reasons, namely around Apple limitations around the data you do and don't have on a user level.

541
00:54:29,760 --> 00:54:32,320
And then also our own privacy layer we had put on top of that.

542
00:54:32,720 --> 00:54:34,720
But like think of creative ways around that.

543
00:54:34,880 --> 00:54:39,760
So one thing we did is we generally D deanonymize like all data because we thought that was the right thing to do.

544
00:54:40,000 --> 00:54:49,200
So instead of giving you necessarily like personalized stats, we would give you stats across the whole user base of we've blocked this many millions of calls this month for our users.

545
00:54:49,600 --> 00:54:56,880
So still be able to demonstrate like we are doing things, we are providing value even if we can't perfectly tie that to like you as an individual user.

546
00:54:57,200 --> 00:54:57,440
All right.

547
00:54:57,520 --> 00:55:08,160
So we've kind of been dancing around the acquisition side of things in talking about LTV and measuring your LTV and like figuring out how your users are engaging with the value of your product.

548
00:55:08,560 --> 00:55:11,040
But then you do have to go out and acquire those users.

549
00:55:11,360 --> 00:55:20,160
So how did you think about that marketing and customer acquisition costs and kind of keeping that in check, especially in relation to LTV?

550
00:55:20,880 --> 00:55:25,440
Yeah, we did think about the world in organic versus paid were two lenses we put on it.

551
00:55:25,680 --> 00:55:33,920
And then I think you can think about your CAC then both from a blended perspective, so basically counting for your organic traction and a paid only perspective.

552
00:55:34,400 --> 00:55:45,920
And I would strongly recommend that you look at both of those metrics because like there are apps that you'll see that are unprofitable if you look at them on a paid only basis, but profitable on a blended basis, which is great.

553
00:55:46,000 --> 00:55:48,560
And that may be the right decision for your app, but that probably means.

554
00:55:49,040 --> 00:55:50,560
you don't have a lot of growth ahead of you.

555
00:55:50,720 --> 00:55:55,040
And the more you spend on paid, the more negative that's going to go and it's just going to drag down your margins.

556
00:55:55,440 --> 00:55:59,120
So that's why it's important to understand it because it influences what you can do going forward.

557
00:55:59,520 --> 00:56:10,240
But looking at how paid impacts the blended, because for some apps, there is a little bit of a network effect where if you spend more, it's also going to juice your organic.

558
00:56:10,400 --> 00:56:14,880
So if you have any kind of like network effect, spending more maybe does balance out.

559
00:56:15,040 --> 00:56:21,040
But for but to the point we've been making throughout this whole podcast is like that's probably the exception.

560
00:56:22,000 --> 00:56:24,640
And for most apps, you're not going to have.

561
00:56:25,760 --> 00:56:27,040
enough of a network effect.

562
00:56:27,520 --> 00:56:37,440
Some of the things that do come into play here, though, is that, you know, as you spend more and bring in more users and get more reviews, you do push yourself up the search results.

563
00:56:37,680 --> 00:56:39,680
And so that's like an important aspect.

564
00:56:40,320 --> 00:56:43,520
But to your point earlier, it's like you also cap that out.

565
00:56:43,760 --> 00:56:54,000
It's like, you know, if you can't spend more than a million dollars a month, maybe you peek at number three result for the most valuable keyword and you just can't push it beyond that.

566
00:56:54,480 --> 00:57:05,120
So kind of watching the interplay between paid and blended is probably how you determine where that's going to a very real effect in the Boba app ecosystem.

567
00:57:05,840 --> 00:57:07,360
I think it has diminishing returns though.

568
00:57:07,520 --> 00:57:08,560
So I think early in your life.

569
00:57:09,200 --> 00:57:18,320
There is a very strong correlation between paid traction and organic growth where as you drive more paid downloads, you can absolutely increase your organic installs along the way.

570
00:57:18,960 --> 00:57:21,680
At some point, you will start to see that flat line though.

571
00:57:21,840 --> 00:57:23,200
And like you'll get diminishing returns.

572
00:57:23,280 --> 00:57:28,880
And for every paid install you're getting, you're getting a smaller and smaller fraction of an incremental organic install as a result.

573
00:57:29,120 --> 00:57:46,720
So again, like understanding that trade off and where you are on that curve is important because then what you're thinking about is like, is all that matters my LTV to CACC on that incremental dollar of paid spend or should I actually be discounting that or maybe applying a premium to the LTV to CAC because I know I'm getting some incremental organic benefit on the other side?

574
00:57:47,200 --> 00:57:48,480
So I do think that's very important.

575
00:57:48,720 --> 00:57:52,960
I also think to the organic side of things like ASO is very critical.

576
00:57:53,360 --> 00:58:03,040
Like I think it is based on some of the data I've seen, it's like over fifty percent of installs go to come through search and go to one of the top three rankings on search.

577
00:58:03,600 --> 00:58:12,400
So it is really critical that you, if you're going to launch a product, understand what are the relevant keywords for your product, understand what is your ability to rank on those keywords.

578
00:58:12,800 --> 00:58:15,280
This comes back to like when you're thinking about a category in the competition.

579
00:58:15,520 --> 00:58:27,360
Like if you say, I need to rank on this keyword and the first three keywords are like hundred million dollar companies with massive budgets, like that's probably a concern that you need to think through and think through how you're going to handle that.

580
00:58:27,520 --> 00:58:29,280
So you're saying I shouldn't build a weather app.

581
00:58:31,440 --> 00:58:33,760
Well, it seems to be kind of going to sound well regardless.

582
00:58:35,360 --> 00:58:36,480
But I do think that that's real.

583
00:58:36,560 --> 00:58:54,480
Whereas like you have to be really thoughtful about what are the keywords that matter and then measure where you're at on those keywords because what you want to understand is one like what's your growth opportunity and how realistic is it you can achieve that but then also also like that tells you where you are in the curve of diminishing returns on your organic lift because of paid spend.

584
00:58:54,720 --> 00:59:04,240
If you're already ranking one, two, three on the keywords you care about, there's only so much more upside you're going to get from your more paid spend other than just like the economics on that paid spend.

585
00:59:04,800 --> 00:59:08,640
So I think that being keyword aware is very important.

586
00:59:09,280 --> 00:59:18,640
I think this goes to a kind of broader strategy though is that I think any app needs to have some leverage in user acquisition.

587
00:59:18,880 --> 00:59:23,920
I've talked about this a lot and we're talking about it through these two specific lenses.

588
00:59:26,160 --> 00:59:30,160
I do think there are maybe ten other potential lenses to look at it through.

589
00:59:30,400 --> 00:59:37,120
AllTrails being a really famous example is like they didn't have to rely as much on Apple search, although they got featured a ton.

590
00:59:37,440 --> 00:59:43,280
They probably did get a ton of Apple search, but like they had a user acquisition advantage in SEO on the web.

591
00:59:43,680 --> 00:59:58,400
If you go search for any trail and they're going to show up, I think there are advantages you can build outside of ASO and paid spend, but you need to understand what that advantage is and what you're building toward.

592
01:00:00,240 --> 01:00:28,720
of apps and especially again, like you said at the very beginning of the podcast, if you're trying to build a new category that people aren't searching for, it's a really hard uphill battle because you have to figure out something outside of the app store that's going to drive people, whether it's like, you know, TikTok organic, whether it's SEO, whether it's, you know, some advantage in paid spend that you're building something unique, but it is hard to go build a real business in the app store around something that people aren't searching for.

593
01:00:29,360 --> 01:00:40,480
And I think a lot of people discount just how much some of these bigger apps benefited from like the Comms, the Headspaces, the My Fitness Pal Pals lose it famously.

594
01:00:40,720 --> 01:00:51,600
I mean, they were at the top result for decade plus or one of the earliest calorie counting apps and rode that ASO wave into this massive business that they are today.

595
01:00:52,000 --> 01:01:00,720
I think that's discounted a lot or not kind of fully understood when you're trying to compete in that calorie counting space or other spaces.

596
01:01:01,600 --> 01:01:09,200
The incumbents have this advantage in that search that is nearly impossible to break in 2024.

597
01:01:09,600 --> 01:01:25,600
So like, what can you bring to the space and leverage and user acquisition outside of ASO or paid, if that's going to be really hard to compete in, or do you have some advantage in paid because ASO is going to be really tough to crack in these bigger categories?

598
01:01:26,160 --> 01:01:26,960
Agree with all that.

599
01:01:27,040 --> 01:01:30,560
I think so we treated things like content and SEO as organic in our view.

600
01:01:30,640 --> 01:01:37,680
So like ASO was kind of one leg of the organic stool, but totally agree, you can get real user acquisition through other legs.

601
01:01:37,840 --> 01:01:39,840
So content was definitely one that worked for us.

602
01:01:40,720 --> 01:01:44,960
Our best example was for RoboKiller, we were the biggest app in the space and in the US.

603
01:01:45,680 --> 01:01:49,680
So therefore, we had a lot of data on spam calls and text in the US.

604
01:01:50,160 --> 01:01:57,760
And we started publishing reports and working with reporters to basically help them report on the iss issue to say, here's how many spam calls Americans are getting every month.

605
01:01:57,840 --> 01:01:59,040
Here's how it's growing.

606
01:01:59,440 --> 01:02:04,160
We worked with the FCC and the FTC to get them data too, because they didn't have great data on all of this.

607
01:02:04,400 --> 01:02:06,800
So we found that we had this proprietary dataset.

608
01:02:07,280 --> 01:02:08,960
People were interested in talking about it.

609
01:02:09,440 --> 01:02:28,320
And it wasn't a direct, we talked to this reporter and we're going to get a bunch of users, but like when you start accumulating multiple Wall Street Journal articles that reference you and multiple Financial Times articles that reference you and you're getting placements on cable news and things like that, that starts building a brand and organic halo and you could absolutely drive significant user acquisition through that.

610
01:02:28,400 --> 01:02:29,680
So we saw a lot of success with that.

611
01:02:29,920 --> 01:02:32,000
To the extent you can replicate it, like definitely do that.

612
01:02:32,480 --> 01:02:36,240
And then community is another one that I think could be very impactful if you could build a community.

613
01:02:36,400 --> 01:02:39,440
I think it's really hard, but I think it can be very impactful.

614
01:02:39,760 --> 01:02:47,600
And I think maybe not the most traditional sense of it, but like Duolingo, I think that was a big part of their early growth was they made it largely free.

615
01:02:48,000 --> 01:02:49,120
It was very gamified.

616
01:02:49,360 --> 01:02:53,440
It became this thing that you talked about you were doing, you did it visibly on like the Subway to Work.

617
01:02:53,600 --> 01:02:54,640
People saw you doing it.

618
01:02:54,960 --> 01:03:01,680
And they created this like real brand awareness by building a very large community of unmonetized users.

619
01:03:01,760 --> 01:03:04,960
And then they figured out the monetization in a much more meaningful way on the other side of that.

620
01:03:05,280 --> 01:03:18,400
So I do think when you think about breakout stories, I actually think the most common trend between them is they have found some very meaningful channel of organic acquisition outside of App Store search.

621
01:03:18,640 --> 01:03:21,840
And that's really what's helped them break through that ceiling we were talking about before.

622
01:03:22,160 --> 01:03:23,760
Yeah, that totally makes sense.

623
01:03:24,240 --> 01:03:32,400
So that kind of took us down multiple rolls, but back to like brass tax tactics on how to think about customer acquisition costs.

624
01:03:32,720 --> 01:03:34,800
What are the specific things you're looking at there?

625
01:03:35,040 --> 01:03:41,840
So we talked about kind of blended and paid CAC, but then what are some of the formulas and other things you look at there?

626
01:03:42,400 --> 01:03:45,680
I think it's important to look at it across your funnel.

627
01:03:45,920 --> 01:03:48,400
There's a couple of different like intersection points you could think about things.

628
01:03:48,880 --> 01:03:54,640
Like, generally what we see is you either think about a cost per installation, a cost per free trial or a cost per subscriber.

629
01:03:55,040 --> 01:04:00,400
I would choose one of those as your primary metric and that's your tie to your monetization strategy.

630
01:04:00,640 --> 01:04:09,760
So, for example, if you're a free app that's mostly advertising driven, you probably want to think about a CPI because you don't have a free trial or a subscriber, so therefore those aren't relevant metrics to you.

631
01:04:10,080 --> 01:04:15,360
If you don't use free trials, cost per free trial isn't super relevant, but if you just go into direct subscriptions, think about that.

632
01:04:15,760 --> 01:04:22,480
If you use free trials, it may benefit you to think about a cost per free trial because that's a more dynamic, quicker metric.

633
01:04:22,640 --> 01:04:26,960
You don't have to wait seven days for them to convert out of the free trial to figure out what your cost per subscriber is.

634
01:04:27,280 --> 01:04:29,280
So just think about it on a cost per trial basis.

635
01:04:29,440 --> 01:04:34,000
Obviously, you have to make sure that your LTV matches whatever you're using as the denominator for your cost.

636
01:04:34,400 --> 01:04:38,560
But I think about like where in the funnel do you want to measure your acquisition cost is important.

637
01:04:38,800 --> 01:04:41,760
And like I said, then do you think about it as paid and blended?

638
01:04:41,920 --> 01:04:45,520
And then even within paid, think about channel specific acquisition costs.

639
01:04:45,920 --> 01:04:55,120
So understanding is meta more expensive than Google or TikTok, for example, and then understanding where you can pull levers and increase your spend on the other side of those.

640
01:04:55,600 --> 01:05:03,600
And then I think more broadly, tactically, where a lot of paid spend has trended in the mobile space is largely black box algorithms.

641
01:05:03,680 --> 01:05:08,080
If you think of Google and Meta are the two biggest platforms for people to spend on, as well as ASA.

642
01:05:08,560 --> 01:05:11,280
But like both Google and Meta basically set a bid and a budget.

643
01:05:11,520 --> 01:05:15,360
And like that's the degree of control you have over those platforms.

644
01:05:15,840 --> 01:05:17,040
So it becomes a huge lever.

645
01:05:17,360 --> 01:05:22,800
And I'm certainly not the first person to say this is creative is a very big lever and you need to be really focused on it.

646
01:05:23,120 --> 01:05:26,080
And so a couple of things we say there is like always be testing creatives.

647
01:05:26,320 --> 01:05:39,520
You should always have some like if you think about the creatives in your campaign and there's different structures or how you do this, but like there should be some percent that are experimental that are unproven and you're just always putting out new ones out there to see what resonates.

648
01:05:40,000 --> 01:05:42,800
And then to one tactical tip I give people a lot is.

649
01:05:43,680 --> 01:05:45,520
rotate designers on your project.

650
01:05:45,920 --> 01:05:51,680
What you often, unsurprisingly, like everyone has, no matter what your profession is, like your own distinct style.

651
01:05:51,920 --> 01:05:56,560
Certain engineers have a certain way that they like code things and like the way their mind works.

652
01:05:56,960 --> 01:06:05,920
Finance guy background, my financial models had a very consistent like theme and flow to them in terms of like how my brain worked and how that translated into a model.

653
01:06:06,400 --> 01:06:12,240
All designers have a pretty like unique and personal like theme or the things that they like to lean into.

654
01:06:12,880 --> 01:06:20,960
And if you keep using the same designer, what you find is like you get in this trap of incremental improvements where what you're really doing is you're changing five percent.

655
01:06:21,440 --> 01:06:24,160
or ten percent of a thing to see if it works a little bit better.

656
01:06:24,560 --> 01:06:29,920
And sometimes it's actually better just to bring in a new designer and just be like, hey, like, this is the product, go wild with it.

657
01:06:30,080 --> 01:06:37,840
Like, obviously, you got to have brand guidelines around it, and it's got to be relatively consistent, but like, actually try something wholesale new and see how that performs.

658
01:06:38,320 --> 01:06:40,640
So we tried to get in a rotation.

659
01:06:41,120 --> 01:06:44,400
We'd actually for our creative teams rotate them across products.

660
01:06:44,480 --> 01:06:48,800
So you'd work on this product for a couple of months, and then you'd rotate to another product for a couple of months.

661
01:06:49,360 --> 01:06:54,000
And that way got us more diversity of the types of creatives that were flowing through there.

662
01:06:54,320 --> 01:07:05,120
And then so we could pick the winners out and start understanding, okay, well, like, this style is resonating, let's do more of that style, as opposed to the style almost being like a fixed thing because it's just the designer's style.

663
01:07:05,760 --> 01:07:09,440
Now, this is a huge can of worms and we do need to wrap up.

664
01:07:09,840 --> 01:07:19,920
So I'll ask for the top tips and the two to three minute answer, but you got to ride the wave of ATT at Mosaic.

665
01:07:20,640 --> 01:07:23,760
So we've been talking cack and measuring your paid spend.

666
01:07:24,560 --> 01:07:27,360
The question people will be shouting back is like, okay, cool.

667
01:07:28,160 --> 01:07:29,360
Those calculations are great.

668
01:07:29,760 --> 01:07:37,600
But like, any top tips on actually putting pen to paper and measuring these things or keyboard to spreadsheet as it were.

669
01:07:38,080 --> 01:07:45,440
How do you actually meaningfully measure both paid spend return and organic return?

670
01:07:46,080 --> 01:07:51,280
The quickest answer is run multiple analyses and look at the aggregate of those analyses.

671
01:07:51,680 --> 01:07:59,520
So like the way I think about it in the post ATT world is like you still have MMPs, they're still going to give you some degree of a breakdown of your acquisition by channels.

672
01:08:00,000 --> 01:08:02,400
Take that at face value, understand what that says.

673
01:08:02,960 --> 01:08:06,960
Then also do a blended analysis and say, like, what does my blended look like?

674
01:08:07,040 --> 01:08:08,000
Am I still okay with blended?

675
01:08:08,320 --> 01:08:15,680
Similarly, just do like paid versus organic and say there's maybe some mix up between channels on a holistic paid basis with the MMPsP is telling me.

676
01:08:15,760 --> 01:08:16,480
Do I feel good about that?

677
01:08:16,640 --> 01:08:18,240
And so like aggregating across those.

678
01:08:18,560 --> 01:08:21,280
I don't think anyone can rely on like one singular dashboard.

679
01:08:21,680 --> 01:08:25,920
I yearn for the days of pre ATT where I had like a Looker report that I fully trusted.

680
01:08:26,000 --> 01:08:30,480
And like I just knew that like this specific geo campaign on Google was doing this.

681
01:08:30,640 --> 01:08:34,400
But like that, those days are gone and like you have to accept that they're gone in my view at least.

682
01:08:34,800 --> 01:08:35,440
So you do that.

683
01:08:35,680 --> 01:08:41,920
And then also think about like operationally how and when you launch and or scale things.

684
01:08:42,160 --> 01:08:50,160
So what I mean by that is like if you say, okay, we don't do any paid spend now and we don't launch on Google, Meta and TikTok, don't do all three at once.

685
01:08:50,800 --> 01:08:55,200
Just launch on Google and then understand, okay., like all we've changed is we're now spending on Google.

686
01:08:55,600 --> 01:08:56,480
What's the impact of that?

687
01:08:56,880 --> 01:08:59,920
Get Google to a steady state and then say, okay, now I'm going to launch on Meta.

688
01:09:00,400 --> 01:09:03,520
And now you're, you have a new baseline and all you've done is change one thing.

689
01:09:04,000 --> 01:09:09,600
If you launch all three at once, it's going to be very hard to understand the relative performance of each and like what's going on there.

690
01:09:10,000 --> 01:09:12,880
So always think about doing things very incrementally.

691
01:09:13,280 --> 01:09:21,920
And the same thing, if you've already launched and you want to start changing bids and budgets, change like one bid, give it a month, see what happens, then change another bid.

692
01:09:22,240 --> 01:09:29,040
And you have to give yourself time to establish baselines that you can do that like incrementality analysis of I made a change.

693
01:09:29,200 --> 01:09:31,920
And now what am I seeing in my aggregate numbers as a result of that.

694
01:09:32,480 --> 01:09:33,920
Yeah, that's super helpful.

695
01:09:34,160 --> 01:09:38,080
And probably the most helpful thing is for folks to just hear it's hard.

696
01:09:38,880 --> 01:09:39,360
Yeah, it's hard.

697
01:09:39,600 --> 01:09:41,040
Like you're always kinda guessing.

698
01:09:41,520 --> 01:09:48,960
And at the end of the day, like I ground myself in blended performance, which is like because like that's your P and L is inherently a blended mix of everything you're doing.

699
01:09:49,120 --> 01:09:52,800
And so like, and at the end of the day, your P and L is what has to make sense, what has to work.

700
01:09:53,280 --> 01:09:58,320
So like that should be your North Star and like your grounding light of does the blended make sense?

701
01:09:58,400 --> 01:10:00,960
And if the answer is no, like you gotta go figure that out.

702
01:10:01,600 --> 01:10:07,520
And then as long as the blended is making sense, then attempt to better understand the dynamics under there.

703
01:10:07,920 --> 01:10:11,200
But like always be grounded in the blended has to make sense.

704
01:10:11,600 --> 01:10:11,680
Yeah.

705
01:10:12,240 --> 01:10:14,320
Well, I think it's a great place to wrap up.

706
01:10:14,480 --> 01:10:16,720
I know people would be hoping for a magic answer.

707
01:10:16,880 --> 01:10:20,000
I feel like I get asked this all the time, like, how do I measure?

708
01:10:20,480 --> 01:10:22,080
Like, which M and P do I use?

709
01:10:22,800 --> 01:10:26,640
And the answer is like, it's just hard and everybody's got to figure it out.

710
01:10:27,120 --> 01:10:28,880
Tell Apple to reverse their change.

711
01:10:30,320 --> 01:10:33,040
Well, I've been pushing hard on improving scan.

712
01:10:33,200 --> 01:10:34,800
I think Apple, well, what is it called now?

713
01:10:34,960 --> 01:10:37,200
I don't even remember the new acronym for it.

714
01:10:37,360 --> 01:10:42,000
